// @description MIDI CC Visualizer
// @author Giorgio Robino
// @version 0.6.0
// @about
//   Real-time visualization of MIDI Continuous Controller (CC) messages with two
//   display modes: a scrolling time graph and current-value bars, plus performance
//   metrics for hardware testing and technique analysis (events/s, rate, jitter).
//
//   Each CC number is tracked per MIDI channel, so the same CC from different
//   channels is displayed and measured separately.
//
//   Slider 1 selects the CC to monitor (0 = all CCs); slider 3 sets the time window
//   in seconds (1-30). Metrics are computed over a 1-second window, filtered on
//   read, so the numbers describe exactly the second they claim to.
//
//   Each (CC, channel) pair has its own colour shade, the bars carry it in the
//   label, the time axis is labelled, and Freeze stops the acquisition so a
//   reading can be examined without it moving.
//
//   The three numbers per pair are named after what they mean, not after how they
//   are computed: Messages = how many times per second the controller sends;
//   Motion = how far the value travels per second (gesture speed);
//   Timing = how much the spacing between messages wobbles. Pointing at them
//   says the same thing on screen.
// @link
//   GitHub https://github.com/solyarisoftware/reaper
//   Issues https://github.com/solyarisoftware/reaper/issues
// @screenshot https://raw.githubusercontent.com/solyarisoftware/reaper/master/MIDI/midi_cc_visualizer.png
// @provides
//   midi_cc_utils.jsfx-inc
//   midi_cc_metrics.jsfx-inc
// @changelog
//   0.6.0 Readable: one colour per (CC, channel) instead of per CC, labelled time
//         axis, metrics that degrade instead of running off the edge, plain value
//         next to each bar, Freeze and Clear, exact 1 s window (counts were up to
//         10% high), 500 events/s per pair instead of 200, jitter that ignores
//         gaps, reports how many intervals it used, and says n/a when there are
//         too few to mean anything.
//   0.5.2 Correctness pass: the (CC, channel) key no longer overflows its memory
//         range, slider 1 filters the graph and the bars together, bars can no
//         longer be drawn under the status line, the metrics block and the
//         event timestamps are sized and taken correctly (jitter was quantised
//         to the audio block and offset by the callback's scheduling).
//   0.5.1 Cleaner bar layout: explicit "Events:/Rate:/Jitter:" labels, no pipe separators
//   0.5   Multi-channel support: CC data, colors and metrics tracked per (CC#, channel)

// ===================================================================
// MIDI CC VISUALIZER WITH REAL-TIME GRAPH AND PERFORMANCE METRICS
// ===================================================================
// Real-time visualization of MIDI Continuous Controller (CC) messages
// with dual display modes: time-based graphs and current value bars
// with integrated performance analysis metrics.
//
// FEATURES:
// - Real-time scrolling XY graph showing CC value changes over time
// - Horizontal bars displaying current CC values (0-127)
// - Performance metrics for hardware testing and technique analysis:
//   * Events per second: MIDI message frequency (hardware resolution)
//   * Rate (u/s): Value change velocity (playing intensity)
//   * Jitter: Timing consistency (hardware quality indicator)
// - Dynamic layout: graph area maximizes available space while bars
//   automatically adjust height based on number of active CCs
// - Unique persistent color per CC from 12-color palette (shared across channels)
// - Configurable time window (1-30 seconds)
// - Monitor all CCs simultaneously or focus on a single CC
// - Full 0-127 value range with grid lines and axis labels
// - Clean, minimal interface optimized for data clarity
// - MULTI-CHANNEL SUPPORT: Visualizes same CC# from different MIDI channels
//
// MULTI-CHANNEL SUPPORT (v0.5+):
// - Each CC# can appear on multiple MIDI channels (1-16)
// - Display format: "CC1 (CH:01)" shows CC number and MIDI channel
// - Sorting: Grouped by CC# then by channel for easy comparison
// - Color scheme: CC#-based coloring (same CC# = same color across channels)
// - Metrics: Tracked independently per (CC#, Channel) pair
// - History: Stores channel info with each data point
//
// PERFORMANCE METRICS EXPLAINED:
//
// Events per Second (Events: X/s):
//   - How many MIDI CC messages arrive per second
//   - High values (60+) = high-resolution controller
//   - Low values (10-20) = coarse updates or slow movements
//   - Useful for: Testing controller resolution and USB timing
//
// Rate (Rate: X u/s - units per second):
//   - How fast CC values are changing (sum of value deltas per second)
//   - Quality ratings: Gentle (0-15) | Moderate (16-40) | Active (41-70) | Aggressive (71+)
//   - Useful for: Analyzing playing technique and gesture intensity
//
// Jitter (Jitter: ±Xms):
//   - Timing consistency between MIDI events (standard deviation)
//   - Quality ratings: Excellent (<5ms) | Very Good (6-15ms) | Good (16-30ms) | Fair (31-50ms) | Poor (>50ms)
//   - Useful for: Hardware quality testing, detecting USB/timing issues
//
// DISPLAY FORMAT (per CC bar - v0.5.1+):
// CC01 (CH:01)  [████████████░░░░░░]  67    Events: 45/s    Rate: 23 u/s (Gentle)    Jitter: ±4ms (Excellent)
//
// CONTROLS:
// - Slider 1: CC to Monitor (0 = All CCs, 1-127 = specific CC)
// - Slider 2: Freeze, stops acquisition (the MIDI keeps flowing through)
// - Slider 3: Time Window in seconds (1-30)
// - Slider 4: Clear history and metrics (resets itself to 0)
//
// CHANGELOG (v0.6.0):
// - One colour per (CC, channel): same hue per CC, different shade per channel
// - The bar label is drawn in that colour, so the rows are their own legend
// - Labelled time axis ("-8s ... now") with a tick interval that follows the window
// - Metrics degrade to shorter forms instead of being clipped by the window edge
// - The CC value is printed right after the bar, without a "Value:" prefix
// - Freeze (slider 2) stops acquisition; Clear (slider 4) resets history and metrics
// - The metrics window is exactly one second, filtered on read: counts were up to
//   10% high because they counted whatever the 100 ms prune had not removed yet
// - 500 events/s per pair (was 200); jitter shows n/a below 5 events in the window
// - The numbers are named after what they mean: Messages (how often it sends),
//   Motion (how fast the value moves), Timing (how much the spacing wobbles)
// - Timing ignores gaps (an interval over 4x the shortest is a pause, not jitter)
//   and says how many intervals it was computed on: "±0.4ms (steady, 240 int)"
// - The quality words are for musicians: slow/moderate/brisk/fast, steady/even/
//   uneven/loose/erratic
// - Pointing at the numbers explains them in a small panel
//
// CHANGELOG (v0.5.2):
// - Bars follow slider 1 like the graph (one shared visible selection)
// - Fixed: every event of an audio block carried the same timestamp, so the
//   reported jitter was quantised to the block length (10.7 ms at 512/48k).
//   Events are now timed at block_start + offset/srate, on an audio-sample
//   clock instead of the wall clock (which jitters with the callback).
//
// CHANGELOG (v0.5.1):
// - Refactored draw_cc_bars() function for improved UI clarity
// - Removed pipe separators (│) for cleaner single-line display
// - Added explicit metric labels: "Events:", "Rate:", "Jitter:"
// - Optimized spacing using natural gaps instead of visual dividers
// - Metrics now immediately identifiable without referencing documentation
//
// CHANGELOG (v0.5):
// - Added multi-channel MIDI support
// - Data structure changed from (cc_num) to (cc_num, channel) pairs
// - History buffer now stores: time, cc_num, channel, value
// - Color assignment remains CC#-based for visual consistency
// - Metrics tracked per (CC#, Channel) pair independently
// - Display shows "CCn (CH:XX)" format in bar labels
// - Bars sorted by CC# then by channel number
// - Refactored utility functions to handle channel parameter
// - Updated metric tracking to account for channel-specific streams
//
// ARCHITECTURE:
// - Modular function-based design for maintainability
// - Separate utility library (midi_cc_utils.jsfx-inc) for data management
// - Separate metrics library (midi_cc_metrics.jsfx-inc) for performance analysis
// - Efficient memory management for history buffer and metrics
// - All metrics calculated over 1-second rolling window for real-time feedback
// ===================================================================

desc:MIDI CC Visualizer with Real-Time Graph and Metrics
author: Giorgio Robino
version: 0.6.0
date: 2026-11-01

// Import utility libraries
import midi_cc_utils.jsfx-inc
import midi_cc_metrics.jsfx-inc

options:no_meter
slider1:0<0,127,1>CC to Monitor (0=All)
slider2:0<0,1,1>Freeze (stop acquiring)
slider3:10<1,30,1>Time Window (seconds)
slider4:0<0,1,1>Clear history and metrics

@init
// ========== CONFIGURATION VARIABLES ==========
FONT_SIZE = 16;          // Text size
BAR_HEIGHT = 8;          // Height of each CC bar
BAR_SPACING = 8;         // Space between bars
MIN_BAR_SECTION = 80;    // Minimum height for bar section
MAX_BAR_PROPORTION = 0.35; // Bars take max 35% of window
Y_AXIS_PADDING = 0.05;   // 5% padding top and bottom for Y-axis
STATUS_TEXT_MARGIN = 25; // Space reserved for the status text at the bottom
BAR_SECTION_TOP_GAP = 4; // Gap between the graph area and the first bar
DOT_RATE_MAX = 15;       // above this many messages/s, no dots on the traces

// ========== GLOBAL LAYOUT VARIABLES ==========
g_graph_height = 0;
g_bars_height = 0;
g_start_y = 0;
g_label_width = 0;
g_margin = 0;
g_available_width = 0;
g_max_bars = 0;
g_visible_bars = 0;
g_graph_start_y = 0;
g_graph_margin_left = 0;
g_graph_margin_right = 0;
g_graph_margin_bottom = 0;
g_graph_width = 0;
g_graph_actual_height = 0;
g_y_range_min = 0;
g_y_range_max = 0;
g_y_range_total = 0;

// ========== TIME WINDOW VARIABLES ==========
// One clock for the whole plugin: the audio timeline, advanced by samplesblock
// in @block. time_precise() would be the wall clock at which the audio callback
// happened to run, and that jitters by about a millisecond between callbacks --
// which is the same order as the jitter this plugin reports (measured: a MIDI
// item with events exactly 8.33 ms apart read 1 ms of jitter with the wall
// clock). Events, the visible window and the metrics all use this clock.
g_clock_sec = 0;         // end of the last processed block, in seconds

g_prev_freeze = 0;       // slider2 edge detection
g_freeze_time = 0;       // clock value when Freeze was engaged
g_prev_clear = 0;        // slider4 edge detection

g_time_window = 0;
g_time_start = 0;
g_time_end = 0;

// ========== METRICS VARIABLES ==========
g_last_prune_time = 0;
PRUNE_INTERVAL = 0.1; // Prune old metrics every 100ms

// ========== FUNCTION DEFINITIONS ==========

// The (CC, channel) key encoding has ONE definition, in
// midi_cc_utils.jsfx-inc: make_cc_channel_key(), key_to_cc(), key_to_channel().

/**
 * Calculates all layout dimensions for the UI with dynamic sizing
 * 
 * This function computes:
 * - Bar section height based on number of active CCs (dynamic!)
 * - Graph section gets ALL remaining space (maximized!)
 * - Bar positioning and spacing (at bottom)
 * - Graph margins and dimensions (at top)
 * - Y-axis range with padding
 * 
 * Results are stored in global g_* variables for use by drawing functions
 * 
 * @param filtered_count Number of (CC#, Channel) pairs to display in bars section
 */
function calculate_layout(filtered_count) local(required_bar_height, max_bar_height, available_bar_space) (

  // Minimum height needed for the bar section: one row per (CC, channel)
  // pair, plus the status line and the gap under the graph.
  required_bar_height = (filtered_count * (BAR_HEIGHT + BAR_SPACING)) + STATUS_TEXT_MARGIN + BAR_SECTION_TOP_GAP;
  required_bar_height = max(required_bar_height, MIN_BAR_SECTION);

  // Ensure bars don't dominate the window (max 35% of total height)
  max_bar_height = floor(gfx_h * MAX_BAR_PROPORTION);
  g_bars_height = min(required_bar_height, max_bar_height);

  // ========== GRAPH GETS ALL REMAINING SPACE (TOP) ==========
  g_graph_height = gfx_h - g_bars_height;

  g_graph_start_y = 10;
  g_graph_margin_left = 35;
  g_graph_margin_right = 10;
  g_graph_margin_bottom = 10;
  g_graph_width = gfx_w - g_graph_margin_left - g_graph_margin_right;
  g_graph_actual_height = g_graph_height - g_graph_start_y;

  // ========== BARS ARE PINNED TO THE BOTTOM, ABOVE THE STATUS LINE ==========
  // The bar section starts where the graph ends (plus a small gap): no magic
  // offsets, so the last bar cannot be drawn under the status text.
  g_start_y = gfx_h - g_bars_height + BAR_SECTION_TOP_GAP;
  g_label_width = FONT_SIZE * 6;  // room for the "CCnn/CHnn" label
  g_margin = 20;
  g_available_width = gfx_w - g_label_width - g_margin * 2;

  // How many bars really fit between the graph and the status line
  available_bar_space = gfx_h - g_start_y - STATUS_TEXT_MARGIN;
  g_max_bars = max(floor(available_bar_space / (BAR_HEIGHT + BAR_SPACING)), 0);
  g_visible_bars = min(filtered_count, g_max_bars);

  // Calculate Y-axis range with padding
  g_y_range_min = 0 - (127 * Y_AXIS_PADDING);
  g_y_range_max = 127 + (127 * Y_AXIS_PADDING);
  g_y_range_total = g_y_range_max - g_y_range_min;
);

/**
 * Calculates the time window parameters in SECONDS
 * 
 * Sets:
 * - g_time_window: Duration of visible time window (seconds)
 * - g_time_start: Start time of visible window (seconds)
 * - g_time_end: End time of visible window (seconds)
 */
function calculate_time_window() local(current_time_sec) (
  // While frozen the window stops at the instant Freeze was engaged, so the
  // graph and the numbers describe the same moment.
  current_time_sec = g_prev_freeze ? g_freeze_time : g_clock_sec;
  
  g_time_window = slider3;
  g_time_end = current_time_sec;
  g_time_start = g_time_end - g_time_window;
);


/**
 * Returns the index-th (CC#, channel) pair of the VISIBLE selection: all active
 * pairs when slider1 is 0, otherwise only the channels carrying that CC#.
 *
 * The graph and the bars must both use this, or the two disagree: before
 * 0.5.2 the bars ignored slider1 entirely and the graph drew nothing.
 *
 * @param index 0-based index into the visible selection
 * @return Combined key (CC#, channel) or -1
 */
function get_visible_cc_channel_pair(index) (
  slider1 != 0 ? get_active_cc_channel_pair_by_cc(slider1, index)
               : get_active_cc_channel_pair(index)
);

/**
 * Draws horizontal bars showing current CC values with performance metrics
 * 
 * For each active/filtered (CC#, Channel) pair:
 * - Draws CC number and channel label
 * - Draws colored bar proportional to CC value (0-127)
 * - Draws "Value: X" label with clear metric identification
 * - Draws performance metrics with explicit labels: Events, Rate, Jitter
 * 
 * DISPLAY FORMAT (v0.5.1):
 * CC01 (CH:01)  [████████████░░░░░░]  67    Events: 45/s    Rate: 23 u/s (Gentle)    Jitter: ±4ms (Excellent)
 * 
 * CHANGES (unreleased, 0.5.2):
 * - Bars follow slider1 like the graph (get_visible_cc_channel_pair)
 * - Bar width is derived from g_available_width, computed once in the layout
 * - A pair that resolves to -1 is skipped instead of drawing garbage
 *
 * CHANGES (v0.5.1):
 * - Removed pipe separators (│) for cleaner appearance
 * - Added "Value:" label to CC value display for clarity
 * - Added explicit metric labels: "Events:", "Rate:", "Jitter:"
 * - Optimized spacing using natural gaps instead of visual separators
 * - Improved readability by clearly identifying each metric
 * - Single-line format maintained for high CC count scenarios
 * 
 * Uses global layout variables set by calculate_layout()
 */
function draw_cc_bars() local(y_pos, i, cc_channel_pair, cc_num, channel, val,
                               bar_x, bar_width, fill, metrics_x, avail,
                               events_ps, rate_ps, rate_q, jitter_ms, jitter_q,
                               tw, th) (
  y_pos = g_start_y;
  i = 0;

  // The bar takes 30% of the row; the rest is the value plus the metrics, which
  // degrade to whatever the window can hold instead of running off the edge.
  bar_width = floor(g_available_width * 0.30);
  bar_width = max(bar_width, 50);

  loop(g_visible_bars,
    cc_channel_pair = get_visible_cc_channel_pair(i);

    cc_channel_pair >= 0 ? (
      cc_num = key_to_cc(cc_channel_pair);
      channel = key_to_channel(cc_channel_pair);
      val = get_cc_channel_value(cc_num, channel);

      // One colour per (CC, channel): the label is drawn in it, so the rows are
      // their own legend and two channels of one CC stay distinguishable
      get_cc_channel_color(cc_num, channel);

      gfx_x = g_margin;
      gfx_y = y_pos + (BAR_HEIGHT - FONT_SIZE) / 2;
      gfx_printf("CC%02d/CH%02d", cc_num, channel + 1);

      bar_x = g_margin + g_label_width;
      fill = (val / 127.0) * bar_width;
      fill = max(fill, 2);
      gfx_rect(bar_x, y_pos, fill, BAR_HEIGHT);

      // The number is the bar written out: no "Value:" prefix
      gfx_x = bar_x + bar_width + 8;
      gfx_y = y_pos + (BAR_HEIGHT - FONT_SIZE) / 2;
      gfx_printf("%d", val);

      events_ps = get_events_per_second_cc_channel(cc_num, channel);
      rate_ps = floor(get_rate_per_second_cc_channel(cc_num, channel));
      rate_q = get_rate_quality(rate_ps);
      jitter_ms = get_jitter_ms_cc_channel(cc_num, channel);
      jitter_q = jitter_ms < 0 ? "" : get_jitter_quality(jitter_ms);

      metrics_x = bar_x + bar_width + 40;
      gfx_x = metrics_x;
      gfx_y = y_pos + (BAR_HEIGHT - FONT_SIZE) / 2;
      avail = gfx_w - metrics_x - 8;

      gfx_r = 0.72; gfx_g = 0.72; gfx_b = 0.72; gfx_a = 1;

      events_ps <= 0 ? (
        gfx_drawstr("no activity in the last second");
      ) : (
        jitter_ms < 0 ? (
          sprintf(#m_full,  "Messages %d/s    Motion %d u/s (%s)    Timing n/a",
                  events_ps, rate_ps, rate_q);
          sprintf(#m_mid,   "Messages %d/s    Motion %d u/s    Timing n/a", events_ps, rate_ps);
          sprintf(#m_short, "%d msg/s   %d mot   n/a", events_ps, rate_ps);
        ) : (
          // n = how many intervals the figure is computed on: five and two
          // hundred are not the same claim
          sprintf(#m_full,  "Messages %d/s    Motion %d u/s (%s)    Timing ±%.1fms (%s, %d int)",
                  events_ps, rate_ps, rate_q, jitter_ms, jitter_q, g_jitter_intervals);
          sprintf(#m_mid,   "Messages %d/s    Motion %d u/s    Timing ±%.1fms (%d int)",
                  events_ps, rate_ps, jitter_ms, g_jitter_intervals);
          sprintf(#m_short, "%d msg/s   %d mot   ±%.1fms", events_ps, rate_ps, jitter_ms);
        );
        sprintf(#m_min, "%d msg/s", events_ps);

        gfx_measurestr(#m_full, tw, th);
        tw <= avail ? (
          gfx_drawstr(#m_full);
        ) : (
          gfx_measurestr(#m_mid, tw, th);
          tw <= avail ? (
            gfx_drawstr(#m_mid);
          ) : (
            gfx_measurestr(#m_short, tw, th);
            tw <= avail ? (
              gfx_drawstr(#m_short);
            ) : (
              gfx_drawstr(#m_min);
            );
          );
        );
      );
    );

    y_pos += BAR_HEIGHT + BAR_SPACING;
    i += 1;
  );
);




/**
 * Draws the graph grid lines without background
 * 
 * Creates:
 * - Horizontal grid lines at CC values: 0, 32, 64, 96, 127
 * - Y-axis value labels on the left side
 * - Clean, minimal appearance without dark background
 */
function draw_graph_background() local(i, level, weight, y) (
  // The plot area is a very slightly lighter panel: it defines the drawing area
  // without a heavy frame, and the margins stay black
  gfx_r = 0.025; gfx_g = 0.025; gfx_b = 0.03; gfx_a = 1;
  gfx_rect(g_graph_margin_left, g_graph_start_y, g_graph_width, g_graph_actual_height, 1);

  // Frame first: the traces are drawn on top of it, so a peak at 127 is not cut
  gfx_r = 0.18; gfx_g = 0.18; gfx_b = 0.20; gfx_a = 1;
  gfx_rect(g_graph_margin_left, g_graph_start_y, g_graph_width, g_graph_actual_height, 0);

  // A scale, not a texture: the two bounds marked, the centre lighter. Five equal
  // lines read as background noise; 0 / 64 / 127 read as a scale.
  gfx_setfont(1, "Arial", FONT_SIZE - 4);

  i = 0;
  loop(3,
    level = i == 0 ? 0 : i == 1 ? 64 : 127;
    weight = level == 64 ? 0.26 : 0.34;
    y = g_graph_start_y + g_graph_actual_height
        - ((level - g_y_range_min) / g_y_range_total) * g_graph_actual_height;

    gfx_r = weight; gfx_g = weight; gfx_b = weight; gfx_a = 1;
    level == 64 ? (
      // centre: dashed, so it cannot be mistaken for a bound
      gfx_x = g_graph_margin_left;
      loop(floor(g_graph_width / 8),
        gfx_rect(gfx_x, y, 4, 1, 1);
        gfx_x += 8;
      );
    ) : (
      gfx_line(g_graph_margin_left, y, g_graph_margin_left + g_graph_width, y);
    );

    // Labels brighter than the grid, right-aligned in the margin, digits padded
    gfx_r = 0.5; gfx_g = 0.5; gfx_b = 0.52; gfx_a = 1;
    sprintf(#ylab, "%3d", level);
    gfx_x = g_graph_margin_left - 30;
    gfx_y = y - (FONT_SIZE - 4) / 2;
    gfx_drawstr(#ylab);

    i += 1;
  );

  gfx_setfont(1, "Arial", FONT_SIZE);
);

/**
 * Draws vertical time grid lines and labels on X-axis
 * 
 * Creates tick marks every 1 second with time labels showing
 * seconds relative to the oldest visible point in the window
 */
function draw_time_grid() local(k, t, x_pos, tick_interval, label_interval, is_major) (
  // A ruler: a faint line every second, a brighter one where there is a label.
  // Ticks are counted *backwards from now*, so the labels read exactly -5s, -10s
  // and stay aligned with the right edge while the window slides.
  tick_interval = 1;
  label_interval = slider3 <= 12 ? 1 : slider3 <= 24 ? 2 : 5;

  k = 0;
  loop(ceil(g_time_window / tick_interval) + 1,
    t = g_time_end - (k * tick_interval);

    t >= g_time_start ? (
      x_pos = g_graph_margin_left + ((t - g_time_start) / g_time_window) * g_graph_width;

      x_pos >= g_graph_margin_left && x_pos <= g_graph_margin_left + g_graph_width ? (
        is_major = (k % label_interval) == 0;

        is_major ? (
          gfx_r = 0.30; gfx_g = 0.30; gfx_b = 0.32;
        ) : (
          gfx_r = 0.14; gfx_g = 0.14; gfx_b = 0.16;
        );
        gfx_a = 1;
        gfx_line(x_pos, g_graph_start_y, x_pos, g_graph_start_y + g_graph_actual_height);

        // Labels only on major ticks, brighter than the grid, above the border,
        // and never in the corner that belongs to "now"
        (is_major && k > 0 && x_pos < g_graph_margin_left + g_graph_width - 38) ? (
          gfx_setfont(1, "Arial", FONT_SIZE - 4);
          gfx_r = 0.52; gfx_g = 0.52; gfx_b = 0.54;
          gfx_x = x_pos + 3;
          gfx_y = g_graph_start_y + g_graph_actual_height - FONT_SIZE - 5;
          gfx_printf("-%ds", k);
        );
      );
    );

    k += 1;
  );

  // Right edge is the present
  gfx_setfont(1, "Arial", FONT_SIZE - 4);
  gfx_r = 0.62; gfx_g = 0.62; gfx_b = 0.64;
  gfx_x = g_graph_margin_left + g_graph_width - 26;
  gfx_y = g_graph_start_y + g_graph_actual_height - FONT_SIZE - 5;
  gfx_drawstr("now");
  gfx_setfont(1, "Arial", FONT_SIZE);
);

/**
 * Draws CC data lines and points on the graph
 * 
 * For each active (CC#, Channel) pair (or selected CC#):
 * - Scans through history buffer
 * - Filters data within visible time window
 * - Draws lines connecting consecutive points
 * - Draws dots at each data point
 * - Draws border around graph area
 * 
 * Uses color assigned to each CC number (shared across channels)
 * 
 * @param visible_count Number of (CC#, channel) pairs in the visible selection
 */
function draw_cc_data_lines(visible_count) local(history_count, cc_idx, cc_channel_pair,
                                                 cc_num, channel, first_point, prev_x, prev_y, hist_idx, base,
                                                 h_time, h_cc, h_channel, h_val, relative_time, x, y,
                                                 draw_dots) (
  history_count = get_history_count();

  // Exactly the same selection the bars use (see get_visible_cc_channel_pair)
  cc_idx = 0;

  loop(visible_count,
    cc_channel_pair = get_visible_cc_channel_pair(cc_idx);

    cc_channel_pair >= 0 ? (
      cc_num = key_to_cc(cc_channel_pair);
      channel = key_to_channel(cc_channel_pair);
      
      // Same colour family as the bar for this (CC, channel)
      get_cc_channel_color(cc_num, channel);
      
      // A 2 px dot per event turns into a 4-5 px band once the events are less
      // than a pixel apart: at 240 evt/s they are 0.37 px apart in a 10 s window.
      // Dots belong to sparse data, where they are the only way to see a sample.
      draw_dots = get_events_per_second_cc_channel(cc_num, channel) <= DOT_RATE_MAX;

      // Scan through history and draw this (CC, Channel) pair's data
      first_point = 1;
      prev_x = 0;
      prev_y = 0;
      
      hist_idx = 0;
      loop(history_count,
        base = get_history(hist_idx);
        base >= 0 ? (
          h_time = mem[base];
          h_cc = mem[base + 1];
          h_channel = mem[base + 2];
          h_val = mem[base + 3];
          
          // Check if this entry matches current (CC, Channel) pair and is within time window
          h_cc == cc_num && h_channel == channel && h_time >= g_time_start && h_time <= g_time_end ? (
            g_time_window > 0 ? (
              // Calculate X position using RELATIVE time from window start
              relative_time = h_time - g_time_start;
              x = g_graph_margin_left + (relative_time / g_time_window) * g_graph_width;
              
              // Calculate Y position with padding
              y = g_graph_start_y + g_graph_actual_height - ((h_val - g_y_range_min) / g_y_range_total) * g_graph_actual_height;
              
              // Draw line from previous point
              first_point == 0 ? (
                gfx_line(prev_x, prev_y, x, y);
              );
              
              // Dot only when the data is sparse enough for it to be a dot
              draw_dots ? gfx_circle(x, y, 2, 1);
              
              prev_x = x;
              prev_y = y;
              first_point = 0;
            );
          );
        );
        hist_idx += 1;
      );
    );
    
    cc_idx += 1;
  );
  
);

/**
 * Draws status information text at the bottom of the UI
 * 
 * Displays:
 * - Waiting message if no data received
 * - Time window settings and history point count
 * 
 * @param filtered_count Number of active/filtered (CC#, Channel) pairs
 * @param history_count Number of points in history buffer
 */
/**
 * Explains the three numbers, but only where they are: with the pointer over the
 * metrics of a row, a small box says what Messages, Motion and Timing are. The
 * window stays clean the rest of the time, and nothing has to be read in the
 * README before the numbers make sense.
 */
function draw_metric_tooltip() local(i, y_pos, hit, metrics_x, bx, by, bw, bh, lh) (
  hit = 0;
  y_pos = g_start_y;
  i = 0;
  loop(g_visible_bars,
    (mouse_y >= y_pos - 2 && mouse_y <= y_pos + BAR_HEIGHT + BAR_SPACING) ? (
      metrics_x = g_margin + g_label_width + floor(g_available_width * 0.30) + 40;
      (mouse_x >= metrics_x) ? hit = 1;
    );
    y_pos += BAR_HEIGHT + BAR_SPACING;
    i += 1;
  );

  hit && mouse_x > 0 && mouse_y > 0 ? (
    bw = 520; bh = 62;
    bx = min(mouse_x + 16, gfx_w - bw - 6);
    bx = max(bx, 6);
    by = mouse_y - bh - 14;
    by < 6 ? by = mouse_y + 22;
    by = min(by, gfx_h - bh - 6);

    // Opaque panel: the rows underneath must not show through. gfx_r/gfx_g/gfx_b/
    // gfx_a is the idiom that works here -- gfx_set(...,1,0,0) left the panel
    // translucent in REAPER 7.82.
    gfx_r = 0.09; gfx_g = 0.09; gfx_b = 0.12; gfx_a = 1;
    gfx_rect(bx, by, bw, bh, 1);
    gfx_rect(bx, by, bw, bh, 1);
    gfx_r = 0.45; gfx_g = 0.45; gfx_b = 0.45; gfx_a = 1;
    gfx_rect(bx, by, bw, bh, 0);

    gfx_setfont(1, "Arial", FONT_SIZE - 4);
    gfx_r = 0.9; gfx_g = 0.9; gfx_b = 0.9; gfx_a = 1;
    // gfx_drawstr advances gfx_x: every line has to set it again
    gfx_x = bx + 8; gfx_y = by + 6;
    gfx_drawstr("Messages  times per second the controller sends");
    gfx_x = bx + 8; gfx_y = by + 23;
    gfx_drawstr("Motion    how far the value travels per second");
    gfx_x = bx + 8; gfx_y = by + 40;
    gfx_drawstr("Timing    how much the spacing between events wobbles");

    gfx_setfont(1, "Arial", FONT_SIZE);
  );
);

function draw_status_info(filtered_count, history_count) local() (
  gfx_r = 0.6; gfx_g = 0.6; gfx_b = 0.6; gfx_a = 1;
  gfx_x = 10;
  gfx_y = gfx_h - STATUS_TEXT_MARGIN + 5;

  filtered_count == 0 ? (
    slider1 != 0 ? (
      gfx_printf("Waiting for CC%d data (any channel)...", slider1);
    ) : (
      gfx_drawstr("Send MIDI CC data to visualize.");
    );
  ) : (
    gfx_printf("%d s window · 1 s metrics", slider3);

    // Never hide bars silently
    filtered_count > g_visible_bars ? (
      gfx_printf(" · showing %d of %d pairs", g_visible_bars, filtered_count);
    );

    g_prev_freeze ? (
      gfx_r = 1.0; gfx_g = 0.6; gfx_b = 0.2;
      gfx_printf("   FROZEN");
    );
  );
);

/**
 * Processes incoming MIDI messages in the audio block
 * 
 * Handles CC messages (0xB0):
 * - Extracts MIDI channel from message
 * - Assigns color to new CCs (based on CC number only)
 * - Stores current CC values per (CC#, Channel) pair
 * - Adds timestamped entries to history buffer with channel info
 * - Updates performance metrics per (CC#, Channel) pair
 * - Passes all MIDI through unchanged
 */
function process_midi_messages() local(offset, msg1, msg2, msg3, status, cc_num, cc_val,
                                        midi_channel, block_start_sec, event_time_sec) (
  // The audio clock: block_start is the time of this block's first sample and
  // advances by exactly samplesblock/srate per block, so consecutive blocks are
  // evenly spaced even when the audio callback is scheduled late. midirecv()'s
  // offset is in samples from block_start, so an event's time is
  // block_start + offset/srate -- the same timebase the visible window uses.
  block_start_sec = g_clock_sec;
  g_clock_sec += samplesblock / srate;

  // Process MIDI messages
  while (midirecv(offset, msg1, msg2, msg3)) (
    status = msg1 & 0xF0;

    // Timestamp of THIS event, not of the block
    event_time_sec = block_start_sec + offset / srate;
    
    // Check if this is a CC message (0xB0)
    status == 0xB0 ? (
      cc_num = msg2 & 0x7F;
      cc_val = msg3 & 0x7F;
      midi_channel = msg1 & 0x0F;  // Extract MIDI channel (0-15)
      
      // Assign color IMMEDIATELY when CC is first received (based on CC number only)
      is_cc_active(cc_num) == 0 ? (
        assign_color_to_cc(cc_num);
      );
      
      // Freeze stops acquisition, not the MIDI stream: what is on screen stays
      // on screen, byte for byte, while the pass-through keeps working.
      slider2 == 0 ? (
        store_cc_channel_value(cc_num, midi_channel, cc_val);
        add_to_history_with_channel(event_time_sec, cc_num, midi_channel, cc_val);
        update_metrics_for_cc_channel(cc_num, midi_channel, cc_val, event_time_sec);
      );
    );
    
    // Pass MIDI through
    midisend(offset, msg1, msg2, msg3);
  );
  
  // Periodically prune old metrics (every 100ms). Not while frozen: the events
  // the frozen window shows must survive.
  slider2 == 0 ? (
    block_start_sec - g_last_prune_time > PRUNE_INTERVAL ? (
      prune_old_metrics(block_start_sec);
      g_last_prune_time = block_start_sec;
    );
  );
);

@block
// Process all MIDI messages in this audio block
process_midi_messages();

@sample
// No per-sample processing needed

@gfx 1000 720
// ========== MAIN RENDERING PIPELINE ==========

// Clear background
gfx_clear = 0;
gfx_mode = 0;

// Set font size
gfx_setfont(1, "Arial", FONT_SIZE);

// ========== SLIDER EDGES ==========
// Freeze: remember the instant, so the window and the numbers describe it
slider2 != g_prev_freeze ? (
  g_prev_freeze = slider2;
  slider2 ? g_freeze_time = g_clock_sec;
);

// Clear: one-shot slider, resets to 0 by itself
slider4 == 1 ? (
  slider4 = 0;
  clear_history();
  init_metrics();
  clear_all_cc_values();
  g_last_prune_time = g_clock_sec;
);

// ========== BUILD SORTED LIST OF ACTIVE (CC#, Channel) PAIRS ==========
active_count = build_active_cc_channel_pair_list();

// ========== APPLY CC FILTER ==========
filtered_count = 0;
slider1 != 0 ? (
  // Count how many channels have this CC#
  filtered_count = count_cc_channels(slider1);
) : (
  // Show all active (CC#, Channel) pairs
  filtered_count = active_count;
);

// ========== CALCULATE DYNAMIC LAYOUT ==========
calculate_layout(filtered_count);

// ========== CALCULATE TIME WINDOW ==========
calculate_time_window();

// The metrics measure against the same "now" the window shows: the buffers are
// pruned every 100 ms, so counting what is buffered would overstate the rate.
g_metrics_now = g_time_end;

// ========== DRAW UI ELEMENTS ==========
draw_graph_background();
draw_time_grid();
draw_cc_data_lines(filtered_count);
draw_cc_bars();

// Get history count for status display
history_count = get_history_count();
draw_status_info(filtered_count, history_count);

// On top of everything: the explanation of the numbers under the pointer
draw_metric_tooltip();
